Static means that the variable or method is shared by all instances of the class. It is not tied to any specific instance of the class. Static members are loaded into memory only once, when the class is loaded, and are shared by all instances of the class.
Static variables are stored in the class's memory space, not in the instance's memory space. This means that all instances of the class share the same static variable.
Static methods are methods that belong to the class itself, not to any instance of the class. They can be called without creating an instance of the class.
Here is an example of a static variable and a static method:
```
public class MyClass
{
    public static int MyStaticVariable = 10;
    public static void MyStaticMethod()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, World!");
    }
}
```
You can access the static variable and method like this:
```
MyClass.MyStaticVariable = 20;
MyClass.MyStaticMethod();
```
Note that you can also use the `this` keyword to access static members, but it's not necessary:
```
MyClass.MyStaticVariable = 20;
MyClass.MyStaticMethod();
```
is equivalent to:
```
MyClass myClass = new MyClass();
myClass.MyStaticVariable = 20;
myClass.MyStaticMethod();
